We can define three vectors. R = <1,0,0> U = <0,1,0> F = <0,0,1>.
R is the x axis motion, which is right/left. U is the y axis motion, or up and down, and F is the z axis motion, or forward/back.
All three vectors are relative to camera position. So the cameras forward facing direction etc...
Now, also note vector addition is simple. <1,0,0> + <0,1,0> = <1,1,0>.

Therefore, we can define our vector like this xR + yU + zF.
This gives us our new vector, which as of now is just <x,y,z>.
Now, we can account for the change in pitch. Lets define phip as the amount of camera pitch.
Since it rotates around the x axis, the x axis remains in place, being R = <1,0,0>.
Now, lets define F. Imagine all axis rotating 45 degrees around F. Now F is pointing 45 degrees upward. (Recall F points in z direction)
The vector F now extends into both the y axis and z axis at an angle phi. Therefore, its y component, assuming its length is 1,
is sin(phip). And its z component, assuming a length of one, is cos(phip).
Therefore, F = <0,sin(phip),cos(phip)>.
Finally, we need to find U.
U starts at 90 degrees and rotates, lets say 45 degrees. Therefore it would have its y as sin(45+90 = 135) and cos(135).
However, with phi, we want to remove that 90. y = sin(90+phip) z = cos(90+phip).  Recall that sin(90-phip) = cos(phip).
Same in reverse. And, cos(phip) = sin(90-phip)
Therefore, 90+phip can be rewritten to sin(90+phip) = cos(90-(90+phip)) This is by adding a 90 to the beginning and negating the term in it.
For example, say it just was theta = 90+phip.  sin(theta) = cos(90-theta) plug back in theta, then cos(90-(90+phip))
Now reduce, and you get cos(-phip).
Same thing for its cos, or z term. cos(90+phip) = sin(90-(90+phip)) = sin(-phip)
Finally, using simple relationships, recall sin is an odd graph and cos is even. Therefore, cos(-phip) = cos(phip), and
sin(-phip) = -sin(phip).
Therefore, the final vector, U = <0,cos(phip),-sin(phip)>

