Now, lets assume cx,cy,and cz are the camera pos. Now lets find the real pos on the xyz graph.
First of all, we dont need y, since we only care about x and z coords. So we can just ignore cy.
Second, we can determine x and z in quite an interesting way. imagine taking a line from the center of the
robot and projecting it along the xz plane to the camera xz coords. Now, imagine the robot is at a certain bearing,
that bearing will rotate the camera around a circle. Therefore, if we find the magnitude between the two coords, the x
and z can be determined by Magnitude and cos or sin of theta, where theta is yaw or bearing of the robot.
However, what if your camera is already at an angle. Simply put, just flip the axis of the xz to match the robot.
Since we are only dealing with x and z coords now, we can assume simple 2D kinematics, as there is no y motion involved in
this.

To get Magnitude, do sqrt(x^2 + z^2) where x = cx - distanceToCenterX, and z = cz - distanceToCenterZ.
Of course, in this, assume you are basing it off the front of the robot, when yaw = 0. And, assume that
you are along your quadrant for positive values. If the camera is on the front of the robot, then
distanceToCenterZ = negative value. The reason is bc my relative Quadrant is quadrant 4. Except both are positive
there. The top left corner is (0,0,0), and the bottom right is (144,0,144). You must account for that. Therefore positive
for me is down and to the right from center.

Now, assuming you have magnitude, it is easy to determine your value. However, note that magnitude will be changed later.
This is bc we are assuming 0 yaw offset rn.
It is just Mcos(yaw2),Msin(yaw2). The sin is the z direction, and the cos is the x direction. yaw2 is a new variable,
defined by true bearing. To get this, we use the yaw equation from earlier with our cx, cz values.

yaw2 now can be found simply by using addition of angles, since it is 2D kinematics.
Firstly, you need your calculated yaw from earlier. That was yaw. Second, you need your camera offset yaw. That was phiy.
Third, you need your tag offset yaw. That was thetay. Finally, since it is all 2D kinematics, you just add. Therefore,
we need to figure out a scenario to base our equation on. Say the april tag is facing toward thetay = 0 degrees.
Now, say the camera is facing toward the april tag. it reads yaw = 180 degrees. therefore, the camera has a 180 degrees
direction it is looking. So, add thetay + yaw. However, now assume the camera yaw is 180 degrees of the robot.
Therefore, the robots real orientation is 0 degrees. Therefore, subtract or add phiy.

yaw2 = thetay + yaw + phiy

Now though, this is an example with 180 degrees. In order to determine whether to subtract or add, we need fancy degrees.
Lets assume your tag is facing 45 degrees. Assume your camera also reads 45 degrees in yaw. Draw a picture and visualize it.
Since the camera must have a positive 45 degree angle from the tag. From the direction your tag is facing, it moves down
45 degrees. Therefore, instead of adding you do thetay - yaw. Though, this may change depending if the angle is measured
clockwise or counterclockwise. Finally, assume the camera is mounted 45 degrees(measured counterclockwise) of the robot.
Now, it looks like the robots yaw is -45 on the drawing. Therefore, subtract phiy.

yaw2 = thetay - yaw - phiy or
yaw2 = thetay + yaw - phiy depending on whether it is detected clockwise or counterclockwise.

Finally, we need to rotate the x z coord plane back to the original one. Finally, we need to flip it back to its original pos.
Since this is 2d kinematics instead of 3D, this becomes relatively simple.
cYawOffset = atan2(distanceToCenterZ/distanceToCenterX)

Since it is 2D kinematics, we can just add/subtract it from the current yaw2.
Therefore, it is
Mcos(yaw2+cYawOffset) Now, when yaw2 = 0, and lets say cYawOffset = 45 degrees. (Just a test number)
When yaw2 = 0, it is Mcos(45). see. This is saying that the x z values you recieved have a total magnitude, but also
are determined by what degree it alr is.

