 This assumes perfect yaw. However, when yaw is 90 degrees, rather than subtracting, you actually add.
 Therefore we have sqrt((cx - distanceToCenterXcos(theta))^2 + (cz - distanceToCenterZsin(theta))^2)
 But theta is unknown rn. So what do we do?
 First of all, you could solve it by just measuring. Therefore, since that is easiest, we will ignore translations,
 and solve it manually.
 But, that actually changes our equation. instead, just do this. sqrt(distanceToCenterX^2 + distanceToCenterZ^2)
 In this, you ignore any rotations, measure the distance, and take its magnitude. No need to overcomplicate it.
 However, again, this changes our equation. Instead of doing all that, we are finding true x and z offset.

 So, simple fix. Relabel these TrueXCoord = Mcos(yaw2 + cYawOffset), TrueZCoord = Msin(yaw2 + cYawOffset)
