However, there is actually an issue. remember the yaw equation.
yaw2 = thetay - yaw - phiy or
yaw2 = thetay + yaw - phiy depending on whether it is detected clockwise or counterclockwise.
Well this actually wont work.
This is bc yaw is from camera's point to tag. Therefore it can have a yaw of 25 degrees, yet be rotated 0 degrees.
This equation will say it has like -25 degrees when the real robot spin is 0. Therefore, we need to make a clever solution
to fix this problem.
Lets define tcyaw as the tags orientation relative to the camera.
The equation is just thetay - tcyaw - phiy.
Now, how do we get tcyaw. Well, although there are probably some advanced math you can do to get it with what we are
already given, there is no need, since there is an april tag function known as ftcPose.yaw which rather than returning
yaw from camera to the tag, returns the yaw relative to the camera. What is the difference. ftcPose.bearing grabs the
yaw from the camera to the tag. But, it returns yaw despite robot turn. it measures a positional vector rather than rotational.
Though it does account for rotational as well. However, this one returns the calculated yaw not based on position, but rotation.

However, this now incorporates a new issue (Also sorry if i keep adding on i am discovering this on the fly...)

But look. Now you may be wondering. What about phiy? Phiy is used directly to find positional offset. However, what if
our robot is rotated at an angle. Is phiy still 180 degrees. And the answer is no. So, therefore we still define
yaw2 = thetay - tcyaw - phiy.
But then we also need to replace phiy with a new variable. Lets call this originalPhiy.
yaw2 = thetay - tcyaw - originialPhiy.
phiy = originalPhiy + yaw2. (Assuming counterclockwise increases degrees).

Now surely everything is accounted for.

